hello steve kaufman here i haven't done  this for a little while so it's about  time i guess  uh guess what i'm going to talk about  language learning again all right  um i just finished reading this book  here  which is by sebastian leitner zoe  lentman  how you  learn how to learn and the way to  success  now he doesn't just talk about language  learning he talks about a variety of  subjects  i enjoyed it because it was a chance for  me to refresh my german i read the book  in german i picked out a bunch of words  that i didn't know that i  entered as a list into link where i was  able to review them and so forth  and lightner is sort of one of the  earliest people to propose these  space repetition systems and his system  consisted essentially of of a box  and the box was divided into i think  four or five compartments and so you had  your cards that you were learning things  on and as you got one right you moved it  into the second compartment and then he  went  through that second compartment and  moved it into the third and the fourth  and the fifth  which is a learning system and probably  a very good one i wouldn't have the  patience to prepare all those cards but  i'm sure if you did it it's kind of a  neat way to  to make sure that you're focusing on  those items that you're having trouble  learning problem is when you go through  a list there are certain things that  you'll learn right away that just click  and certain things that no matter how  often you review them they just don't  stick  so he applies this to language learning  the book has a lot of stuff in it about  you know the role of of different  researchers on how the brain learns  things and remembers things and  talks about pavlov's dog and how animals  were trained to behave in certain ways  it reviews  you know the influence of behaviorist  skinner and others on  the development of  learning theory  i must say i didn't end up getting that  much out of the book  some of the things that he says that i  agree with are the idea that the more  meaning that there is in in a word the  more likely you are to remember it so  i mean i remember words when i'm  learning languages  because i i identify them with a phrase  i identify them with a particular  context or perhaps with several contexts  so that  as i rem as i review as i do in link the  different phrases where they occurred  and i'm reminded of those different  episodes  then that seems to help me remember them  but inevitably there are some words that  you just can't remember no matter how  many times you review them  and others this  sometimes relatively unimportant words  that click in right away  um  i can't remember all the stuff that was  in there  yeah i mean you need to invest some time  some time that's why again at link we  recommend that people edit the phrases  that we capture automatically the more  time you invest and try to hide that  sunbeam the more time that you invest in  in an item and thinking about it the  more uh the more likely is it is that  it'll stick yet he contradicts this at  one point he suggests that in in working  with uh flash cards we should also work  with them very quickly so that we get a  almost a split second  uh  time lapse between seeing let's say the  word that we're trying to learn and then  seeing what it means in our language and  i've been doing that a little bit with  link where i've been going through our  flash cards sort of hyper fast  click click click click click click to  to create that visual connection within  a fraction of a second between the word  i'm trying to learn  and the meaning or the hint that i've  put in there  editorially to tell if if that's  effective or not  the uh you know i kind of end up with  where i started and that is that  that the what i most like doing  in language learning is listening and  reading  i really enjoy reading on link because i  can click on words right away and i can  see what they mean and i can put them  into a database for review later on  i like being reminded every now and  again as  now that link we're sending out these um  emails with my saved link so that i can  flash card them that helps remind me  when i i'm busy doing something else and  all of a sudden the email shows up and i  have to go through my flash cards so  that's good but the most enjoyable  activity is listening and reading i've  just  loaded up my uh  my little uh ipod shuffle here  with some kuprin  and i'm about to charge off on a run and  listen to russian and that's kind of the  most fun and there'll be all kinds of  words that i don't know and when i come  back i'll read them on link and and save  them and  so i guess i guess the idea is that that  we can't really treat things that we're  trying to learn as as  sort of neutral  inanimate things that have no meaning  they have meaning  the more meaning they have the better  and the more one of the things that  lightner says which is very true is the  more words you have the more words  you can learn because obviously you know  more of the context the surrounding  context you have more words that are  perhaps similar to the word that you  have saved i know that in russian and in  german if i look at my words  alphabetically i'll see a whole bunch of  words that are obviously related to each  other so you start  even if you don't guess the word right  away  uh when you do look it up and you  remember oh yeah that mean well yeah it  means that because actually it's similar  to this other word so the more words you  have that help connect it the better  one of the reasons we ended up with this  term link for our system is that  language learning is all about linking  you're linking sounds to meaning you're  linking words  to a context you're linking a word to a  phrase to an episode that you remember  you're linking words to other words that  are similar you're creating all these  connections and the more words you know  the more things there are there that are  going to help you  link connect  and and and retain the meaning so that  that is certainly true so  at first you see these uh new words very  often because they're very high  frequency and as you keep learning of  course you're learning rarer and rarer  words but on the other hand you already  have so many words that it's easier it's  easier to learn them  um  i did uh let's see how much time i've  got left here i did a  discussion with my two grandchildren two  of my grandchildren who are in french  immersion and we talked about some of  the things that they do for language  learning and one of the things that  teachers do is is to get them to think  in terms of strategy  to learn french words so they get a  french sentence and the teacher decides  which are the difficult words and then  the student or the pupil here eight  years old has to write in the meaning of  that french word and then put a number  beside it number five if they identified  the meaning because of the picture  number four because of the  context number three because there was a  small word in the big word that helped  them  recognize the word number two was  because they knew it before and number  one was because it's similar to english  so they put a number  which i'm sure for the kids is fun but  i really wonder how  how useful it is to  get people to think in such a  self-conscious way  about their reading activity  my granddaughter said that when she  reads in french if she doesn't  understand she just continues reading  which is exactly what i do  if i'm reading away from the computer if  i can't look it up i don't understand it  i read on even if i have all the words  and i still can't make sense of it i  just read on and i i think there's a big  argument in favor of just letting people  read letting people listen letting  people get used to it look up words  review the words occasionally as a sort  of a stimulus as a massage for the brain  not necessarily because you're going to  nail those things down  and  to the extent that kids  identify words that are similar to  others or  infer the meaning from the context or  get it from the picture just let them do  that  why would you ask them to identify which  of these strategies they're using why  would you make them self-conscious about  how they are  enjoying their reading and how they're  deriving meaning from them from the  reading so i've had i've been on this  subject again and i guess i always come  back to this basic idea that if you can  enjoy what you're doing you will learn  and do whatever helps you enjoy  and  so  i've read lightner  uh  but i still think that what we're doing  at link uh makes a lot of sense but hey  everybody has their own different things  some people have other systems they use  there's anki out there there's  super memo and stuff whatever people  enjoy doing is great that was my more or  less review of lightner's book
 

